.TH CLOG_SET_FORMAT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_set_format \- change the output format at runtime
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

ccol_retval_t clog_set_format(clog logger, clog_format_t fmt);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Updates the output format of the shared backing store.  The format
lives in the shared store and not in the struct of each logger, so
the change is visible at once to every logger handle that writes to
the same file descriptor, the root logger and every derived logger.

There are three formats.

  CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT (0)
    The default.  Every record is one logfmt line:
      ts=<ISO-8601-UTC> level=<L> proc=<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>)
      src=<file>:<line> func=<fn> [fields] msg=<text>
    clog_error, clog_alert and clog_fatal append backtrace
    continuation lines after the main record, each indented by a tab.
    In proc, <tname> and <tid> are the name and the ID of the thread
    that wrote the record.  Each thread reads them once, the first
    time it logs, and ccol_set_thread_name(3) renames a thread and
    updates that name.  A value or a message that holds a space, an
    '=', a '"', a backslash, a control character or a byte that is
    not well-formed UTF-8 goes in double quotes.  A control character
    becomes \en, \er, \et or \exNN, a C1 control (U+0080 to U+009F)
    becomes \eu0080 to \eu009f, and each byte that is not part of a
    well-formed UTF-8 sequence becomes U+FFFD, so every record is
    valid UTF-8.

  CLOG_FMT_JSON (1)
    The library writes each record as one NDJSON object followed by a
    newline:
      {"ts":"...","level":"...","proc":"name(pid):tname(tid)",
       "src":"file:N","func":"...", [fields,] "msg":"..."}
    Each structured field becomes a top-level JSON key.  For
    clog_error, clog_alert and clog_fatal the library embeds the
    backtrace as a "bt" array of strings inside the same JSON object.
    Before it embeds a string value (the message, and the key and the
    value of every field), it checks that value as UTF-8 and replaces
    a byte or a sequence of bytes that is not well-formed UTF-8, one
    invalid byte at a time, with the Unicode replacement character
    (U+FFFD).  It writes a control character, the C1 controls U+0080
    to U+009F included, as a \euNNNN escape (\en, \er and \et for
    those three).  So a message or a field value built from arbitrary
    or binary data can never give a record with invalid Unicode
    content.

  CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG (2)
    The RFC 5424 syslog format, which only a logger on an fd offers
    (one from clog_create_from_fd or clog_create_from_fd_mp).  On a
    logger that sits on a file, clog_set_format() with CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG
    returns ccol_invalid_args and the format stays as it is.

    The library writes each record as one line:
      <PRI>1 TIMESTAMP HOSTNAME APP-NAME PID MSGID [ccol ...] MSG\en

      PRI        = (facility * 8) + RFC 5424 severity (mapped from
                   clog_level_t: TRACE=7, DEBUG=7, INFO=6, WARN=4,
                   ERROR=3, ALERT=1, FATAL=0)
      TIMESTAMP  = ISO 8601 UTC (same format as logfmt/JSON)
      HOSTNAME   = the result of gethostname(2).  The library keeps
                   only the bytes that are RFC 5424 PRINTUSASCII
                   (0x21-0x7e) and drops every other byte, a space
                   and a control character included, without
                   truncating the value at that point.  It writes
                   "-" when this leaves no byte at all, or when
                   gethostname(2) itself fails.
      APP-NAME   = the name of the executable (the basename of
                   argv[0]), filtered to PRINTUSASCII in the same
                   way.  The library writes "-" when this leaves no
                   byte at all.
      PID        = getpid(2)
      MSGID      = level string ("INFO", "ERROR", etc.)
      [ccol ...]  = RFC 5424 SD-ELEMENT with SD-ID "ccol" carrying
                   proc="<name>(<pid>):<tname>(<tid>)",
                   src="<file>:<line>", func="<fn>", and any fields
                   that clog_set_field() attached
      MSG        = the formatted message text

    In MSG, and in every value of proc, src, func and a field, the
    library escapes a control character, a literal newline and a
    carriage return included, with a backslash as \en, \er, \et or
    \exNN, and a C1 control (U+0080 to U+009F) as \eu0080 to
    \eu009f.  It writes a backslash itself as \e\e, so the escaped
    text reads back without ambiguity: a real newline appears as \en,
    and the two characters '\e' and 'n' appear as \e\en.  logfmt uses
    the same scheme for its own values.  In MSG and in every
    SD-PARAM-VALUE (the values of proc, src, func and the fields), the
    library passes a well-formed UTF-8 sequence through unchanged and
    replaces each byte that is not part of one with U+FFFD, as the
    JSON format does, because RFC 5424 requires an SD-PARAM-VALUE to
    be UTF-8.  MSG carries no byte order mark, so it is the MSG-ANY
    form of RFC 5424, whose bytes a receiver must not assume to be
    UTF-8, even though the library writes only valid UTF-8 there.
    HOSTNAME and APP-NAME can hold no byte that needs this escape,
    because the filter above takes both down to PRINTUSASCII.  So the
    content of a record can never split that record across several
    lines.

    clog_set_facility() sets the facility, which defaults to
    CLOG_SYSLOG_USER.  The library writes a backtrace (which
    clog_error, clog_alert and clog_fatal produce) as separate syslog
    messages, one for each frame, and each of them carries the same
    PRI, TIMESTAMP and MSGID as the original record.  The MSG of a
    frame message is "#<n> " followed by the text of frame n, escaped
    with a backslash in the same way as MSG.  Every frame shares the
    TIMESTAMP of the original record instead of carrying its own write
    time, and that is what lets a reader match a record with its own
    backtrace frames.

    Connect the fd to a syslog daemon before you call
    clog_set_format(); on Linux that is usually a SOCK_DGRAM Unix
    socket at /dev/log.  Keep each message under 2 KiB to stay inside
    the usual datagram limits of syslogd.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
logger  Logger handle.
fmt     CLOG_FMT_LOGFMT, CLOG_FMT_JSON, or CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success       The format is set.
ccol_invalid_args  clog_format_t does not name fmt (such as 9), or fmt
                   is CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG and the logger sits on a file.
                   The format stays as it is, and clog_get_format(3)
                   keeps reporting it.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Thread-safe, because the mutex of the shared backing store protects the
call.
Asserts when logger is CLOG_NULL, stale or already closed.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR clog_get_format (3),
.BR clog_set_facility (3),
.BR clog_create_from_fd_mp (3),
.BR clog_create_from_file_mp (3),
.BR clog_derive (3),
.BR ccol_set_thread_name (3)
